---
title: Gallery & lightbox
description: A grid of pictures that opens into a dark full-screen viewer — a counter, zoom, the original, arrows, a caption and a strip of every picture. The thumbnails are plain links to the large image, so it works before, and without, the script.
eyebrow: Media
source: src/components/gallery.css · src/js/im-media.js
---
<h2>Gallery</h2>
<p>Click any picture.</p>
{{#example}}
<div class="im-gallery" data-im-lightbox>
	{{#foreach demo.six}}<a class="im-gallery-item" href="{{feature_image}}" data-caption="{{title}}"><img src="{{feature_image}}" alt="{{title}}" loading="lazy"></a>{{/foreach}}
</div>
{{/example}}
<p>In the viewer: <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> move, <kbd>Home</kbd> <kbd>End</kbd> jump, <kbd>z</kbd> zooms, <kbd>Esc</kbd> comes out of a zoom and then closes, a swipe moves on touch, and a click in the dark around the picture closes it.</p>
<p>It is a <strong>dark theatre in both themes</strong> — a photograph is judged against black. Along the top: where you are (<code>03 / 12</code> and the site), zoom, the original in a new tab, close. Press the picture to zoom into the point you pressed; move to look around. Along the foot: the caption, then a strip of every picture in the set, the current one ringed, to jump straight to any of them. On a phone the arrows go (swipe) and the strip gets smaller.</p>
<p>It is one native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, built the first time it is needed, so focus is trapped, the page behind cannot scroll, and focus returns to the thumbnail afterwards — all from the browser. <kbd>Esc</kbd> is caught rather than left to the browser, so the viewer leaves the way it arrived. Neighbouring images are warmed in the background so the arrows feel instant.</p>

<h2>The frame mark</h2>
<p>Every picture that opens carries the <strong>same mark</strong> in its top-right corner: four corner brackets, a frame. A gallery tile, a single picture, a slide in a carousel, a photograph in the middle of a post — one thing to learn, not four. It sits at 72% and comes up to full under the pointer.</p>
<div class="im-table-scroll">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th></th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>im-gallery-item</code></td><td>A tile in a gallery.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>im-zoomable</code></td><td>A single picture that opens — the link <em>wraps</em> the image.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>im-zoom-link</code></td><td>What <code>im-content.js</code> wraps each picture in a post body with.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>im-zoom-cover</code></td><td>A link that <em>covers</em> a picture instead of wrapping it — for markup that cannot take a wrapper, such as the post hero's feature image.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>--im-expand-idle</code></td><td>0.72. Set it to <code>0</code> to hide the mark until the picture is hovered; <code>--im-expand-size</code> and <code>--im-expand-inset</code> are the other two knobs.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Layouts</h2>
{{#example title="im-gallery-mosaic — the first picture large"}}
<div class="im-gallery im-gallery-mosaic" data-im-lightbox>
	{{#foreach demo.six limit=5}}<a class="im-gallery-item" href="{{feature_image}}" data-caption="{{title}}"><img src="{{feature_image}}" alt="{{title}}" loading="lazy"></a>{{/foreach}}
</div>
{{/example}}
{{#example title="im-gallery-masonry — pictures keep their own shape"}}
<div class="im-gallery im-gallery-masonry" data-im-lightbox style="--im-gallery-min: 11rem">
	{{#foreach demo.ten limit=7}}<a class="im-gallery-item" href="{{feature_image}}" data-caption="{{title}}"><img src="{{feature_image}}" alt="{{title}}" loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio: {{#if @even}}4 / 5{{else}}{{#if @first}}1 / 1{{else}}16 / 10{{/if}}{{/if}}; object-fit: cover"></a>{{/foreach}}
</div>
{{/example}}
{{#example title="im-gallery-strip — one row that scrolls and snaps"}}
<div class="im-gallery im-gallery-strip" data-im-lightbox>
	{{#foreach demo.six}}<a class="im-gallery-item" href="{{feature_image}}" data-caption="{{title}}"><img src="{{feature_image}}" alt="{{title}}" loading="lazy"></a>{{/foreach}}
</div>
{{/example}}
<div class="im-table-scroll">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>--im-gallery-min</code></td><td>12rem — the narrowest a column may be</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>--im-gallery-gap</code></td><td>0.5rem</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>--im-gallery-ratio</code></td><td>1 / 1 — the thumbnails' shape (grid layouts)</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>A single picture</h2>
<p><code>data-im-lightbox</code> on one link opens just that picture — for a feature image, or any image in an article.</p>
{{#example style="max-width: 30rem"}}
<figure>
	<a class="im-zoomable" data-im-lightbox href="{{demo.post.feature_image}}"><img src="{{demo.post.feature_image}}" alt="{{demo.post.title}}" loading="lazy"></a>
	<figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted text-center">The caption is read from the figure's own figcaption.</figcaption>
</figure>
{{/example}}

<h2>In a carousel</h2>
<p>A slide is a picture like any other: put <code>data-im-lightbox</code> on the track and make each slide an <code>im-zoomable</code> link. The viewer then holds the whole set, in the carousel's order, and the arrows in the viewer step through it — which is the point, because a carousel only ever shows three of eleven.</p>
{{#example}}
<div data-im-lightbox>
	{{#> "components/carousel" label="Pictures from the road" style="--im-slides: 1.4; --im-slides-sm: 2.4; --im-slides-md: 3.4"}}
		{{#foreach demo.six}}<a class="im-zoomable" href="{{feature_image}}" data-caption="{{title}}"><img src="{{feature_image}}" alt="{{title}}" loading="lazy" class="im-ratio-4-3 im-w-full" style="object-fit: cover"></a>{{/foreach}}
	{{/"components/carousel"}}
</div>
{{/example}}
<p>The attribute goes on a wrapper <em>around</em> the carousel, not on the track: the track sizes its own direct children, and anything between it and the slides takes that away from them.</p>

<h2>With Ghost's own galleries</h2>
<p>Ghost's editor outputs gallery and image cards as plain <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>s, not links. <code>im-content.js</code> does the rest: put <code>data-im-zoom</code> on the post body and every picture in it becomes an <code>im-zoom-link</code> — the frame mark in its corner, the viewer on a click, the figure's caption carried across.</p>
{{{{code "hbs"}}}}
<section class="im-content im-content-lede" data-im-zoom>{{content}}</section>
{{{{/code}}}}
<p>It is one attribute, and it is the whole of it. See <a href="/collections/post/single/">a post, whole</a>.</p>

<h2>Captions and alt text</h2>
<ul>
	<li>The viewer's caption comes from <code>data-caption</code>, else the enclosing <code>&lt;figcaption&gt;</code>, else the image's <code>alt</code>.</li>
	<li><code>alt</code> is carried to the large image, so write it on the thumbnail.</li>
	<li>With a modifier key held — <kbd>⌘</kbd>/<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>-click — the link opens in a new tab as links do; the lightbox steps aside.</li>
</ul>
